e pointed in the most costly direction available. FIXED: bench/runner_argv_guard.py, applied to all 15, proved by BLOCKING ALL SOCKETS FIRST so a failure of the guard would cost nothing, then confirming --help exits with usage and zero outbound attempts while a valid still passes through untouched. Pinned by 23 tests asserting the guard is the FIRST statement of each entry point, because anything before it can reach a paid model; falsified by reverting one runner and confirming red. THE PUREST INSTANCE OF THE PATTERN YET: ONBOARDING.md's 'Last updated' stamp matched HEAD's commit time exactly, so it looked independently corroborated. update_timestamp() rewrote it unconditionally while the body update returned early whenever hand-written prose sat between the markers -- which it always does. The stamp recorded when sv RAN, not when anything was WRITTEN, in the one line designed to tell a reader how fresh something is. Stamping now happens AFTER the body update so it can report what actually occurred, and says so in the line when the body was preserved. SIX DOCUMENTS REPAIRED with dated correction blocks in the file's own convention; no historical entry rewritten. Every command written into a document was EXECUTED first, and that rule caught four places the repair list was itself wrong -- including one that would have told a researcher the project's central resolution rule fails on 60% of cases, when reverify_falsifier simply requires an absolute path. 13 pinning tests DERIVE their truth rather than restating it: active runner read out of launcher_core by AST, credential set out of cdsfl_onboard, experiments-with-results out of bench/logs -- so a move to a v3 runner turns them red automatically. Falsified with 11 deliberate mutations, 11/11 red. THE GAP WAS ROUTING, NOT KNOWLEDGE: every config is committed and the working command was verified end to end, but appears ZERO times in README, PAPER, REPRODUCING, ARCHITECTURE, GLOSSARY or ONBOARDING -- only in prose written